<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">German cruiser Blücher</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> was an Admiral Hipper-class heavy cruiser of the German navy, commissioned in 1939. On 9 April 1940, leading the force sent to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> during the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, she was sunk in the Drøbak Narrows by guns and torpedoes from the old Oscarsborg fortress. The delay allowed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vii">Haakon VII</a>, the government and the gold reserves to escape the capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-cruiser-blucher.jpg" alt="German cruiser Blücher" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klein, A. · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_DVM_10_Bild-23-63-09,_Kreuzer_%22Bl%C3%BCcher%22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Blücher was the second of five Admiral Hipper-class heavy cruisers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kriegsmarine">Kriegsmarine</a>, built after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> came to power in 1933 and repudiated the disarmament clauses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-german-naval-agreement">Anglo-German Naval Agreement</a> of 1935 allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> five cruisers of 10,000 long tons, a limit the Hippers nominally met but greatly exceeded, at over 16,000 long tons. The ship was named for Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher, the Prussian victor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>, and was laid down at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> on 15 August 1935, launched on 8 June 1937 and commissioned on 20 September 1939. She was armed with eight 20.3 cm guns in four twin turrets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Blücher was declared ready for service on 5 April 1940 and assigned to the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Rear Admiral Oskar Kummetz boarded as commander of Group 5, whose task was to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, together with 800 troops. The group left port on 8 April and was seen at night by the Norwegian patrol boat Pol III, whose radio alarm warned the defences.</p><p>At 04:21 the old 28 cm guns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscarsborg-fortress">Oscarsborg Fortress</a> opened fire at very close range in the Battle of Drøbak Sound and hit Blücher twice; the second shell started a fire that detonated explosives carried for the infantry. At 04:34 two torpedoes from a concealed battery struck the boiler and turbine rooms. A fire later reached a 10.5 cm magazine, which exploded, and Blücher capsized and sank at 07:30. Estimates of the dead vary: Henrik Lunde gives 600 to 1,000 soldiers and sailors, while Jürgen Rohwer gives 125 seamen and 195 soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The loss of Blücher and damage to the cruiser Lützow made the German force withdraw. Troops landed on the eastern side of the fjord and took Oscarsborg by 09:00 on 10 April, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> was in German hands by 14:00 that day, but the delay allowed the Norwegian government and royal family to escape the city. The wreck lies at a depth of 64 metres in the Drøbak Narrows. Leaking oil led to an operation in October 1994 that removed 1,000 tonnes and recovered one of her Arado 196 seaplanes. The wreck was protected as a war memorial and war grave on 16 June 2016 to protect it from looters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “German cruiser Blücher” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April 1940 the guns and torpedoes of Oscarsborg sank the German cruiser <b>Blücher</b>, delaying the capture of Oslo, but the country was occupied until May 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li></ul></section>
